# Testing

Tests are the evidence the master plan demands. A passing build is not completion (§11.3).
**Everything gets tests**: every public function, every trait impl, every error path, every bug fix.

## Coverage gates (enforced in CI with `cargo llvm-cov`)
| Scope | Line coverage | Region/branch coverage |
|---|---|---|
| Foundation + domain + bridge crates | ≥ 95% | ≥ 90% |
| Infrastructure + platform crates | ≥ 90% | ≥ 85% |
| Workspace total | ≥ 90% | — |

- New or changed code may not lower a crate's coverage. Uncovered lines need a written justification.
- Coverage is necessary, not sufficient: foundation/domain crates also run mutation testing
  (`cargo mutants`) on a schedule; surviving mutants are triaged as missing assertions.

## Test layers
| Layer | Location | Purpose |
|---|---|---|
| Unit | `#[cfg(test)] mod tests` beside the code | Pure logic, invariants, edge cases |
| Property | `proptest` in unit or `tests/` | Parsers, serializers, coercions, round-trips |
| Trait contract | generic `fn <trait>_contract<T: Trait>(..)` | LSP: every impl (real + fake) passes the same suite |
| Integration | `crates/<name>/tests/` (M-INTEGRATION-TESTS) | Public API across modules |
| Differential | `scenarios/differential/` run by `apps/test-orchestrator` | Native output vs reference WordPress/PHP oracle |
| Fuzz | `fuzz/` (cargo-fuzz, outside the workspace) | Untrusted input: PHP source, HTTP, serialized data, SQL placeholders |
| Browser / load | `e2e/` (Playwright, load tools) | Admin, editor, frontend workflows; SLOs |

A virtual workspace does not compile a root-level `tests/` directory — never put Rust tests there.
Shared fakes, comparators and scenario types live in `crates/test-harness` (dev-dependency only).

## Ground truth
- Expected values come from the reference oracle, official `.phpt` files, or WordPress source — never from
  re-running the code under test (M-TAUTOLOGICAL-TESTS).
- Record where each expected value came from (contract ID, oracle capture, upstream file/line).
- Normalize only proven nondeterminism, each with a written reason (master plan §7.2).
- Ladder A (PHP semantics), B (native Core), C (bridge) are separate suites; never count one as another.

## Determinism
- Inject clock, RNG seed, filesystem and network through ports; tests use fakes, not wall-clock or internet.
- No `sleep` for synchronization; use explicit signals or a controllable clock.
- Tests are order-independent and parallel-safe; each gets its own fixture DB/schema/tempdir.

## Fakes over mocking frameworks
- Prefer small hand-written fakes that implement the port trait and pass its contract suite.
- Test utilities and fakes ship behind a `test-util` cargo feature, never in default builds (M-TEST-UTIL).

## Hygiene
- Test names state the behavior: `filters_run_in_priority_then_insertion_order`.
- Arrange / act / assert; one behavior per test.
- `unwrap()`/`expect()` are fine in tests. `#[ignore]` requires a linked ticket and reason; a skipped
  required test is a failure for gating purposes.
- Every bug fix lands with a regression test reduced to the smallest reproducer.

## Reporting
Completion reports list the exact test commands run and their pass/fail counts. Never claim a test passed
without running it (AGENTS.md rule 16).
